# keur security control — C/C++ shell execution with interpolated input.
#
# Why this exists: keur's OWN platform layer shipped a `tool_on_path` that did
#     std::string cmd = "command -v " + tool + " >/dev/null 2>&1";
#     return std::system(cmd.c_str()) == 0;
# — a textbook OS command-injection shape (CWE-78). SonarQube rated the new code
# 'D Security' on exactly this. The pre-existing KEUR-SEC-003 only covers PHP
# ($_GET into exec) and KEUR-SEC-004 covers dynamic exec() in scripting langs, so
# C/C++ shell interpolation slipped through. This rule closes that gap:
# fix + control (never just the fix).
#
# What it flags:
#   system()/popen() whose argument is a NON-LITERAL (a variable, incl.
#   `var.c_str()`) — the command was built elsewhere and may carry shell
#   metacharacters.
# A shell call with a FIXED string literal (e.g. system("git --version")) does
# NOT match and is allowed.
#
# Scope note: this rule guards the libc sinks only (system/popen). keur's OWN
# process adapter (Proc::run/.capture -> platform::run/capture) is a separate
# sink class, guarded by KEUR-SEC-006. An earlier second pattern here flagged
# any `x = "lit" + var` assignment context-free, which mis-fired on non-exec
# std::cout builders (false positive); that heuristic is removed and the real
# detection now lives on the sink itself in SEC-006.
#
# Prefer a shell-free primitive (posix_spawn/execvp with an argv vector, or an
# access()-based PATH scan) routed through keur::platform. Where a shell is truly
# required, the command must be a fixed literal with no interpolated input.
#
# NOTE: unlike the KEUR-PORT-* rules, common/ is NOT excluded — the platform
# layer is exactly where this shape appeared and must be held to it too.
# @see rules/security/KEUR-SEC-003 (PHP sibling), ADR-005.
id: KEUR-SEC-005
title: Shell execution with interpolated (non-literal) input in C/C++
category: cmdi
severity: critical
engine: pattern
action: block
skip_comments: true
target:
  extensions: .c .cpp .cc
  exclude_paths: vendor/ ../rules/ _test.cpp test/ tests/ build/ dist/
patterns:
  # Flag system()/popen() whose first argument is not a plain "..." literal
  # (a variable, concatenation, or *.c_str()) — a constructed command that may
  # carry shell metacharacters (CWE-78). `exclude` (pattern-not, ADR-007) drops
  # the safe case where the argument IS a bare string literal.
  - regex: \b(?:std::)?(?:system|popen)\s*\(
    exclude: \b(?:std::)?(?:system|popen)\s*\(\s*"
    message: "system()/popen() invoked with a non-literal (constructed) command — OS command injection risk (CWE-78). Build the command shell-free (posix_spawn/execvp with an argv vector, or an access()-based PATH scan) via keur::platform. Reserve system()/popen() for fixed string literals. @see KEUR-SEC-005, ADR-005"
confidence: 0.85
fix: "Replace shell interpolation with a shell-free call: resolve tools with an access()-based PATH scan, spawn processes with posix_spawn/execvp and an argv array (no /bin/sh), exposed through keur::platform. Reserve system()/popen() for fixed literal commands with zero interpolated input. See ADR-005 and KEUR-SEC-005."
